{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval_new/seed2085404187/3/codegen_prompts/7.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2085404187/3`\n- 题号：`7`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval_new/seed2085404187/3/7.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"7\">\n- `description`：在网格中找到被\"遮罩块\"部分覆盖的底层图案，利用相同图案在其他位置的未遮挡副本来还原被遮盖的部分，输出仅包含还原后的遮罩区域。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。\n  - 网格中有若干个彩色图案（每个图案由单一非 black 颜色绘制，形状各异，如 T 形、L 形等），每种图案至少出现两个副本，且副本之间形状完全相同（可能经过平移但未旋转或翻转）。\n  - 有一个 grey 实心矩形\"遮罩块\"覆盖在网格上，部分遮盖了一个或多个图案的某些像素。被遮盖的像素在输入中显示为 grey。\n  - 未被遮盖的图案副本完整可见。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一层推理（识别被遮盖的图案）：找到 grey 遮罩块的位置。检查遮罩块边缘处哪些彩色像素被截断（即某个彩色连通块的一部分与遮罩块相邻，说明该图案被部分遮盖）。\n  - 第二层推理（通过副本还原）：对于每个被遮盖的图案，在网格其他位置找到同色且形状匹配的完整副本。将副本的形状对齐到被遮盖图案的可见部分，从而推断出被 grey 覆盖的那些像素原本应该是什么颜色。\n  - 输出为仅包含 grey 遮罩块区域的子网格（裁剪到遮罩块的 bounding box），其中 grey 像素被还原为正确的底层颜色，未被任何图案占据的 grey 像素还原为 black。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要理解 grey 块是一层\"遮挡\"而非实体对象，然后利用远处的完整副本作为\"参考答案\"来重建被隐藏的信息。这要求同时进行模式匹配（找到对应副本）和空间推理（对齐并填补缺失部分）。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/7b3084d4, train/57edb29d, eval/f560132c, train/8dab14c2, eval/62593bfd, train/9f669b64, eval/fc7cae8d, train/465b7d93\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4046\nmechanism=0.4903\nlexical=0.0130\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:rotate, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:between, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/7b3084d4.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/7b3084d4.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Assemble colored rectangular sprites like puzzle pieces using the grey-cornered orange block as the anchor, fitting other sprites by matching their edge positions.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"colored rectangular sprites without grey corner marker s\",\n  \"dst\": \"anchor sprite (orange block with grey corner pixel) or previously attached sprites d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"edge(s) aligns_with open_edge(d) such that union forms contiguous_tiled_rectangle\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"rotate(anchor, until grey_pixel at top_left)\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"grid_shape\": \"tight crop\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"repeat merge until all sprites assembled into single rectangle\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"jigsaw\",\n    \"assembly\",\n    \"anchor\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The grey corner pixel identifies the anchor sprite and determines output orientation (grey stays at top-left corner). Each sprite is a solid colored rectangle; they tile together to form a complete rectangular output.\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3436\nmechanism=0.4262\nlexical=0.0116\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:shape_match, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:aligned, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/57edb29d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/57edb29d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"edge-relative marker run s in a marked rectangle aligned with the plain rectangle row band\",\n  \"dst\": \"run d in the plain rectangle crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.nearest_edge_offsets == d.nearest_edge_offsets\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each matched d with the shared marker color; keep base rectangle fill elsewhere\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as the plain rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3741\nmechanism=0.4209\nlexical=0.0110\ncode_feature=0.7143\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:rotate, operation:shape_match, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:between, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/f560132c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f560132c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Assemble scattered sprites into a composite shape using a legend sprite that specifies colors for each component position.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"monochrome sprites scattered in the grid s\",\n  \"dst\": \"assemble into composite shape matching the legend sprite's silhouette d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.shape matches d.region.shape (d is always in top-left, final assembly forms rectangle)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.color = legend[s.position_in_composite].color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"legend sprite contains 2x2 colored pixels where each pixel's position (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right) maps to corresponding sprite's recolor in final assembly\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"tight crop\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: identify legend sprite (contains multi-color 2x2 pattern); Step 2: identify other monochrome sprites with same shape structure; Step 3: assemble sprites into composite matching legend silhouette; Step 4: recolor each assembled region based on legend position mapping\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"jigsaw\",\n    \"color-legend\",\n    \"assembly\",\n    \"spatial-correspondence\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3177\nmechanism=0.3882\nlexical=0.0115\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:aligned, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/8dab14c2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8dab14c2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"one thick monochrome foreground object on a dominant background, with one-pixel boundary tabs and missing edge pixels\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each foreground tab, remove the opposite edge pixel on the same row or column; for each missing edge pixel, add a foreground tab just outside the opposite edge, ignoring gaps immediately beside a tab source\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3151\nmechanism=0.3592\nlexical=0.0117\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:rotate, operation:shape_match, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3209\nmechanism=0.3510\nlexical=0.0114\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:translate, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9f669b64.json\n</paths>\n<description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
